  :read-the-ratio-not-the-absolute
  "Neo4j is UNCHANGED code between these two runs and got 1.6-4.9x faster on its own, because the first run was on a host at load average ~190 and this one at ~30. Its absolute numbers are the host, not Neo4j. The kotobase/Neo4j ratio, measured interleaved in one JVM under the same conditions, is the load-invariant column and the only one worth comparing across runs."
  :result
  {:ic09 "85.3x -> 35.8x. The two-hop expansion is 2.4x closer to Neo4j."
   :ic08 "97.5x -> 53.3x. 1.8x closer."
   :ic07 "6.2x -> 7.0x. Flat, and at 7.1 vs 1.0 ms the absolutes are small enough that this is inside the noise."
   :ic02 "7.7x -> 14.7x. WORSE. This is a regression introduced by these changes, not noise -- see below."}}
 :regression
 {:query :ic02
  :what "kotobase improved 2.26x while Neo4j improved 4.31x on the same host improvement, so relative to the yardstick IC02 got about 1.9x worse."
  :suspected-cause
  "IC02 is [?person knows ?f] [?msg hasCreator ?f] [?msg date ?date]. Its hasCreator step sees ~882 distinct keys against 7,355 rows, which is above the 4x budget, so it never hash joins -- it only pays the group-by that batching added. When every binding substitutes to a DISTINCT pattern, that group-by builds a map with one entry per binding and buys nothing: the batching win is exactly the ratio of bindings to distinct patterns, and IC02 has none."
  :suspected-fix
  "In join-clause, when (count groups) equals (count bindings) there was nothing to dedup -- take the original per-binding path and skip building the map. Cheap to test: the answer must be unchanged by construction."
  :status "NOT fixed in this receipt. Named, measured, and attributed rather than left for someone to rediscover."}
 :still-open
 ["IC09 at 35.8x and IC08 at 53.3x are better, not good. Neo4j answers IC08 in 0.7 ms."
  "The [?f1 knows ?f2] cardinality probe still materializes 361,246 rows on every query to learn a number; :query-statistics would skip it."
  "The limit is still applied to the finished projection, so a top-20 query still joins everything -- every one of these four queries is a most-recent-N."
  "Still a 210,389-element subset, 40% of the scale NamiDB publishes against. A 540K run on this now-quiet machine is the one that would make the NamiDB comparison possible."]}
